    {
      "id": "h1-a-i-aloud",
      "difficulty": 4,
      "skills": [
        "sy-memory",
        "sy-os"
      ],
      "tags": [
        "latency-ladder"
      ],
      "pace": 300,
      "kind": "longform",
      "title": "From a[i] to a register",
      "prompt": "Say aloud, in two minutes, what happens from `a[i]` in your code to the value arriving in a register. Time yourself, and use this level's round figures. Then open the model answer and tick the rubric.",
      "model": "**1. The address is virtual.** `a[i]` becomes the address of `a` plus `8 * i` for a double. That address is virtual: a page number and a 12-bit offset into a 4 KiB page.\n\n**2. Translation.** The core looks the page up in the TLB. A hit costs almost nothing. A miss walks the four-level page table: up to four memory reads, about 400 ns if none is cached.\n\n**3. Cache lines.** With the physical address, the core asks for the 64-byte line that holds it. Eight doubles share a line, so a loop in order misses once per eight reads.\n\n**4. The levels.** L1 answers in about 1 ns, L2 in about 4 ns and L3 in about 20 ns. If all three miss, main memory answers in about 100 ns. The line is copied in, evicting the least recently used line of its set.\n\n**5. The slow cases.** If the page has no frame yet, the access faults. A minor fault costs about 1 µs. One that reads from an SSD costs about 100 µs, and from a spinning disk about 10 ms.\n\n**6. Why it matters.** The same `a[i]` can cost 1 ns or 100 µs: five powers of ten. So layout and access order decide speed: keep hot data small, contiguous and in the order you walk it.",
      "rubric": [
        {
          "points": 2,
          "text": "Says the address is virtual and is translated to a physical one through the page table"
        },
        {
          "points": 2,
          "text": "Names the TLB: a hit is nearly free, a miss walks up to four levels, about 400 ns"
        },
        {
          "points": 2,
          "text": "Says memory moves in 64-byte lines, so neighbouring data arrives together"
        },
        {
          "points": 2,
          "text": "Gives L1, L2, L3 and DRAM with round figures of 1, 4, 20 and 100 ns"
        },
        {
          "points": 1,
          "text": "Mentions a page fault or a miss to disk, with its cost"
        },
        {
          "points": 1,
          "text": "Closes with why layout and access order matter"
        }
      ]
    },
